        {
          "type": "tool_use",
          "name": "apply_patch",
          "input": "*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /home/leon0399/Projects/llame/docs/research/chat-search/2026-08-25-episodic-memory-implementation-review.md\n+# Episodic memory after #194: implementation review, alternatives, and decision points\n+\n+Status: noncanonical research memo  \n+Date: 2026-08-25  \n+Scope: issue [#194](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/194), sub-issues [#197](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/197), [#198](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/198), and adjacent issues that affect invocation, consent, and evaluation\n+\n+## Executive position\n+\n+The main risk in the current plan is not weak retrieval. It is weaker than that: treating semantic retrieval artifacts as if they were evidence. If llame retrieves a Russian conversation through an English query and then quotes translated, summarized, or vector-derived text as if it were the original message, it has violated its own source-of-truth rule. Confidence: high.\n+\n+The umbrella issue is directionally right about the hard boundary: canonical chats/messages remain authoritative, while search and vector indexes are derived and rebuildable. The open gap is that [#197](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/197) and [#198](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/198) still underspecify how semantic retrieval becomes a trustworthy citation. That gap matters more than another 5 points of Recall@10. Confidence: high.\n+\n+My recommendation is to keep the current raw-history-first architecture, but tighten it around five decisions:\n+\n+1. Keep raw message content as the only quotable source. Search text, translated text, generated chunk context, and embeddings are retrieval aids, never citation authority.\n+2. Make hybrid retrieval a candidate-generation step, then hydrate canonical message windows and select excerpts from raw text only.\n+3. Reject user-visible \"partial vector match\" as an evidence model. If you want narrower quotes, add a second-stage span selector over canonical text, not fake token-level evidence from a dense vector.\n+4. Separate relevance scoring from evidence quality and from recency. Raw cosine is not a user-facing confidence signal.\n+5. Evaluate invocation and cross-lingual recall explicitly. The latest note on [#194](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/194) is right that invocation, not raw retrieval, is the binding constraint.\n+\n+## Repo-grounded findings\n+\n+### 1. The repo's source-of-truth boundary is already correct\n+\n+[SPEC.md](../../../SPEC.md) says chats, runs, messages, and events are the episodic record, while hybrid chat search is a rebuildable projection over canonical user and assistant text only. It explicitly excludes summaries, tool receipts, model-switch metadata, and other derived material from the episodic corpus. That is the right contract for trustworthy memory. Confidence: high.\n+\n+[search-projection](../../../openspec/specs/search-projection/spec.md) is also correct to keep projection rows rebuildable and to carry message-range pointers rather than treating the projection itself as authoritative content. The decisive implication is this: semantic recall can find a source candidate, but only canonical message content can be quoted back to the model or user as historical evidence. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### 2. #194 is stronger on product framing than on retrieval internals\n+\n+[#194](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/194) now has the right high-level split:\n+\n+- awareness and invocation: shipped recency digest [#307](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/307), pending pull-side recall helpers [#327](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/327) and chat-scoped search [#331](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/331)\n+- retrieval quality: chunking [#517](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/517), embeddings [#196](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/196), hybrid fusion [#197](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/197)\n+- memory semantics: provenance, temporal filters, framing, and recency [#198](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/198)\n+\n+That decomposition is good. The blind spot is that the provenance contract is still described mostly as fields on a result, not as a verified pipeline from semantic match to canonical excerpt. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### 3. #197 is a solid baseline but is still missing the \"vector-only hit -> exact quote\" story\n+\n+[#197](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/197) currently proposes:\n+\n+- synchronous query embedding\n+- exact vector scan\n+- lexical + trigram + vector fusion via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)\n+- weighted top-N document aggregation per chat\n+- `matchedBy` plus message range returned to callers\n+\n+This is a credible first retrieval phase. RRF is a good default for combining heterogeneous rankers because it works on ranks instead of pretending raw scores are calibrated across systems ([Cormack et al., 2009](https://research.google/pubs/reciprocal-rank-fusion-outperforms-condorcet-and-individual-rank-learning-methods/)). Confidence: high.\n+\n+The problem is downstream of that. \"Best document supplies the snippet + message range\" is adequate for lexical hits. It is not adequate for vector-only hits or cross-lingual hits. A vector match can tell you which chunk is relevant. It cannot by itself justify which sentence to quote as the evidence span. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### 4. #198 is directionally right but still incomplete\n+\n+[#198](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/198) gets the important things right:\n+\n+- temporal filters should be explicit and tool-level, not natural-language parsing in the backend\n+- recency should be a ranking signal, not retention or deletion\n+- recalled text must be framed as historical data, not new instructions\n+- episodic recall is separate from semantic/factual memory\n+\n+What it still does not specify:\n+\n+- how to derive an exact excerpt when retrieval succeeded through meaning rather than lexical overlap\n+- whether translated or generated retrieval text can ever appear in a citation\n+- how to expose score/confidence without misleading the model\n+- whether provenance is at chat level, chunk level, message range level, or exact span level\n+\n+That is the missing contract this memo focuses on. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### 5. The current tool surface makes invocation and result shape more important than another retrieval leg\n+\n+`search_conversations` currently takes only `query` and `limit`, and its description is the main trigger for whether the model uses it at all ([apps/api/src/tools/search-conversations.ts](../../../apps/api/src/tools/search-conversations.ts)). That reinforces the latest [#194 comment](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/194#issuecomment-5408762741): invocation is the binding constraint. Confidence: high.\n+\n+It also means two things:\n+\n+- adding many optional parameters too early will not help if the model still does not call the tool\n+- the returned result shape needs to be clean enough that the model can trust and use it without hallucinating over it\n+\n+## Similar systems: what to steal and what to reject\n+\n+### obra/episodic-memory\n+\n+Current README positioning is straightforward: local semantic search over Claude Code and Codex conversations, exposed as search/read tools and backed by local embeddings plus SQLite ([obra README](https://github.com/obra/episodic-memory/blob/main/README.md)). The current surface exposes semantic or exact-text search, time filters, and a separate `read` step for the full conversation. Confidence: high.\n+\n+What it validates:\n+\n+- verbatim transcripts as the memory source, not an extracted-fact store\n+- separate `search` and `read` tools instead of stuffing large transcript blobs into every result\n+- metadata filters like `after` and `before`\n+- description-tuned recall dispatch as a product lever, not just retrieval quality\n+\n+What it does not validate:\n+\n+- ranking quality. Its current surface is much simpler than llame's planned hybrid retrieval.\n+- citation safety. The project still treats retrieved transcript content as ordinary context rather than specifying a hardened recall-time framing contract.\n+- multi-tenant correctness. It is a local single-user system.\n+\n+Verdict: useful product analogue, weak retrieval analogue, bad safety precedent if copied naively. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### Mem0\n+\n+Mem0's recent episodic-memory writeup is useful because it says the quiet part out loud: episodic memory needs timestamp, scope, surrounding context, and type metadata, and repeated episodic items should consolidate into more durable semantic memory over time ([blog](https://mem0.ai/blog/episodic-memory-for-ai-agents)). The API/docs also show scoped search with filters, thresholds, and optional reranking ([search docs](https://docs.mem0.ai/core-concepts/memory-operations/search), [add docs](https://docs.mem0.ai/api-reference/memory/add-memories)). Confidence: high.\n+\n+What to steal:\n+\n+- explicit scope dimensions like `user_id`, `agent_id`, `run_id`\n+- type/category metadata\n+- reranker as an explicit second-stage option\n+- consolidation from repeated episodic records into semantic memory\n+\n+What not to copy as llame's transcript-recall layer:\n+\n+- Mem0's default object is a promoted memory item, not a canonical chat transcript span.\n+- it is optimized for extracted or curated memory objects, not for exact replay/citation from raw message history.\n+\n+Verdict: good supplement for future semantic memory, not a substitute for transcript-grounded episodic recall. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### Letta\n+\n+Letta's docs now make the split explicit:\n+\n+- memory blocks are always-visible, prompt-resident, persistent context sections\n+- archival memory is stored as passages and searched separately\n+- cloud message search exposes FTS/vector ranks and a total RRF score\n+\n+Sources: [memory blocks](https://docs.letta.com/guides/core-concepts/memory/memory-blocks/), [archival memory passages](https://docs.letta.com/api/typescript/resources/agents/subresources/passages), [message search](https://docs.letta.com/api/typescript/resources/messages/methods/search). Confidence: high.\n+\n+This is useful because it matches the architecture llame is converging toward:\n+\n+- small always-loaded state for stable preferences or policies\n+- searchable archival state for episodic recall\n+- hybrid retrieval over searchable messages\n+\n+Verdict: strong confirmation that \"always in context\" and \"episodic archive search\" should remain separate surfaces. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### Zep / Graphiti\n+\n+Zep's Graphiti paper argues for a temporally-aware knowledge graph that synthesizes conversational and structured business data while maintaining historical relationships, and reports gains on Deep Memory Retrieval and LongMemEval ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13956)). Confidence: moderate.\n+\n+What is valuable here:\n+\n+- temporal validity as a first-class concern\n+- explicit handling of historical relationships instead of overwriting facts\n+\n+What is expensive:\n+\n+- schema design\n+- extraction and graph mutation on every turn\n+- graph shape drifting toward early-domain assumptions\n+\n+Verdict: useful derived layer for future event/fact memory, too heavy to make canonical for transcript recall in llame now. Confidence: high.\n+\n+## Research findings that should change the implementation\n+\n+### 1. Cross-lingual retrieval is not \"multilingual benchmark score, therefore solved\"\n+\n+Two papers matter here.\n+\n+LAReQA argues that strong cross-lingual retrieval is a distinct problem: semantically related cross-language pairs must be closer than unrelated same-language pairs ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.05484)). Confidence: high.\n+\n+The Cross-Lingual Cost shows that cross-lingual RAG retrieval degrades substantially when query and supporting document differ in language, and that query translation or balanced multilingual retrieval can recover meaningful performance ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07543)). Confidence: moderate.\n+\n+Implication for llame: multilingual embeddings are necessary but not sufficient. A Russian chat found from an English query is still a harder ranking problem than a same-language hit. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### 2. Multilingual dense models are good enough for phase 1 of semantic recall\n+\n+Two realistic open-weight families are well supported:\n+\n+- multilingual-e5, trained on 1 billion multilingual text pairs, with an instruction-tuned variant ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05672))\n+- BGE-M3, which supports dense, sparse, and multi-vector retrieval across 100+ languages and reports strong multilingual and cross-lingual retrieval results ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03216))\n+\n+This supports llame's current \"exact lexical first, multilingual dense second\" plan. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### 3. A reranker is the most credible next step if hybrid retrieval still underperforms\n+\n+There is now direct conversational-memory evidence for a recall-preserving rerank cascade. ConvMemory v2 reranks only the protected top-10 candidates and improves MRR and hit@1 without changing Recall@10 ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.10842)). Confidence: moderate.\n+\n+That is exactly the kind of second-stage tool llame should consider after [#197](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/197) if vector+lexical fusion still produces noisy top hits. Confidence: moderate.\n+\n+### 4. LongMemEval is the right external benchmark to optimize around first\n+\n+LongMemEval covers information extraction, multi-session reasoning, knowledge updates, temporal reasoning, and abstention, with 500 evaluation instances and both session-level and turn-level evidence annotations ([repo](https://github.com/xiaowu0162/longmemeval)). Confidence: high.\n+\n+That aligns better with llame's needs than retrieval-only metrics. The latest [#194 comment](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/194#issuecomment-5408762741) is therefore sensible: evaluate the end-to-end system and decompose into invocation rate plus correctness given invocation. Confidence: high.\n+\n+I did not independently verify the repo note that LoCoMo should be treated only as a smoke test. That stays an internal repo judgment, not an externally confirmed conclusion in this memo. Confidence: low.\n+\n+## Alternatives and tradeoffs\n+\n+| Approach | What it buys | What breaks | Recommendation |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| Raw transcript search only (FTS/trigram/title) | Best provenance fidelity, simplest ops, easy quoting | weak paraphrase and cross-language recall | Keep as baseline, not enough alone |\n+| Hybrid chunk retrieval plus canonical parent hydration | Best balance of quality, provenance, and implementation cost | needs a second-stage excerpt selector | Recommended now |\n+| Extracted episodic event records with provenance | Better temporal/type reasoning, easier semantic consolidation | extraction errors, schema lock-in, still cannot replace transcript quotes | Add later as supplement |\n+| Temporal knowledge graph | strongest structured temporal reasoning | highest complexity and mutation cost | Defer |\n+| Always-in-context memory blocks | stable preferences/policies always visible | not an episodic archive; easy to overstuff prompt | Use only for non-episodic stable state |\n+\n+## Recommended architecture\n+\n+### 1. Separate canonical source from retrieval representations\n+\n+Each indexed retrieval unit should point back to immutable canonical source data and may carry multiple derived representations:\n+\n+- canonical source: raw message parts and stable message ids\n+- lexical representation: normalized text for FTS/trigram\n+- embedding representation: text actually embedded\n+- optional retrieval aids: translated text, model-generated chunk context, summary labels\n+\n+The canonical source is quotable. Everything else is only searchable. Confidence: high.\n+\n+This implies a stricter row contract than today's \"content + normalizedContent + message range\":\n+\n+- `source_message_ids` or exact source span boundaries\n+- `source_content_hash`\n+- `retrieval_text_kind`\n+- `retrieval_text_language`\n+- `retrieval_text_hash`\n+- `embed_input_version`\n+\n+If a search hit came from translated or generated retrieval text, the result still points to the original message span. Confidence: high.\n+\n+### 2. Retrieve children, hydrate parents\n+\n+Do not answer directly from the best embedding chunk. Retrieve a child unit, then hydrate the parent evidence window.\n+\n+Recommended granularity:\n+\n+- child retrieval unit: chunk or sentence window optimized for recall\n+- parent evidence window: contiguous canonical turn/message window optimized for quoting and reading\n+\n+This is the practical compromise between \"one vector per whole chat\" and \"pretend a single dense vector identifies an exact sentence.\" Confidence: high.\n+\n+### 3. Add an explicit excerpt-selection stage over canonical text\n+\n+For each top hydrated candidate:\n+\n+1. fetch canonical raw text for the matched message range\n+2. split into sentence or clause spans with exact offsets\n+3. score spans against the query using lexical overlap plus a multilingual reranker or lightweight semantic scorer\n+4. choose the smallest contiguous exact substring that still explains the hit\n+5. verify that the final excerpt is an exact substring of canonical raw content before returning it\n+\n+This stage is what turns \"semantic retrieval succeeded\" into \"here is the exact historical evidence.\" Confidence: high.\n+\n+### 4. Never expose vector similarity as model-facing confidence\n+\n+Use a multi-stage score model:\n+\n+1. candidate generation: independent retrieval legs\n+2. fusion: RRF over ranks\n+3. group/hydration: aggregate by chat or episode\n+4. rerank: multilingual cross-encoder or equivalent on canonica